(Review Article: Sleep and Memory Consolidation) Tj
T* (Cognitive Neuroscience - term paper) Tj
T* (Introduction) Tj
T* (Decades of research show that sleep does more than rest the body: it actively stabilizes) Tj
T* (and reorganizes memories formed during the day. This review summarizes evidence for the) Tj
T* (role of different sleep stages.) Tj
T* (Slow-wave sleep and declarative memory) Tj
T* (During deep non-REM sleep, the hippocampus replays recent experiences in compressed form.) Tj
T* (These replay events coincide with sleep spindles and slow oscillations in the cortex, and) Tj
T* (are thought to transfer facts and events into long-term cortical storage. Participants who) Tj
T* (sleep after learning word pairs recall more of them than those who stay awake for the) Tj
T* (same period.) Tj
T* (REM sleep and emotional and procedural memory) Tj
T* (REM sleep appears to support skill learning and the processing of emotional experiences.) Tj
T* (Some studies suggest REM strips the emotional charge from memories while keeping content.) Tj
T* (Sleep deprivation) Tj
T* (A single night without sleep reduces the ability to form new memories by roughly 40) Tj
T* (percent, because the hippocampus cannot encode new information efficiently.) Tj
T* (Targeted memory reactivation) Tj
T* (Playing sounds or odors during slow-wave sleep that were present during learning can) Tj
T* (selectively strengthen those memories.) Tj
T* (Practical implications) Tj
T* (Students cramming all night trade sleep for study time and remember less. Short naps) Tj
T* (containing spindle-rich sleep can also improve recall.) Tj
T* (Limitations) Tj
T* (Many studies use small samples and laboratory tasks; real-world learning is less studied.) Tj
